                                                                          Project condition

The condition of a project shows ***how well the project is going***: on track, in danger, or in serious trouble.
Condition = the health of the project.

The three standard values:
On Target	 ----> The project is on track
At Risk	----->Something could cause a delay or problem if it is not addressed.  -- Example: The client has not approved the design, so development might be delayed.
In Trouble --->The project has a serious problem that may prevent it from meeting its deadline or objectives. -- : Development is significantly delayed, and the launch date is likely to be missed.

How the condition is set

Progress status(Automatic)	----->- Workfront determines the condition based on the project's progress compared with its planned schedule.
Manual	 ---> A person with permission to manage the project sets it themselves

One rule
Projects in the Idea, Requested, Approved and Rejected statuses always show On Target, whatever the dates are. The condition only means something once real work starts.


                                                                    Progress status

Progress status is a field that tells you whether the work is on schedule or not. Workfront calculates it automatically by comparing the dates. You don’t set it by hand.

On Time	------->Work is on schedule
Behind	------->Work is slipping behind the plan
At Risk	------->Work may miss its date
Late	--------->The date has been missed

diff between progress vs condition?

Progress status is the measurement. Workfront calculates it from the dates.
Condition is the health signal people see and report on. It is based on the measurement when automatic, or set by a person when manual.

                                                                       Task Dependencies
A task dependency is a rule that says one task cannot start (or finish) until another task does. The task that must come first is called the predecessor

it has two main parts
Predecessor: the task that must happen first. Another task depends on it.
Successor: the task that must wait. It can only go ahead after its predecessor.

Write the email	Predecessor	It comes first
Send the email	Successor	It waits for the writing to be done

The four dependency types:
1)Finish-to-Start (FS) : the successor starts only after the predecessor finishes.
Example: 2FS+4d means  FS	The type is Finish-to-Start (this task starts after task 2 finishes)  +4d	Add 4 days of waiting (lag) after it finishes

2)Start-to-Start (SS) : the successor can start once the predecessor has started. It does not have to wait for the predecessor to finish.
Predecessor starts → then successor can start.

3)Finish-to-Finish (FF) : The successor can finish only after the predecessor has finished. The two tasks can run together, but they must end in the right order.
Predecessor finishes → then successor can finish.

4)Start-to-Finish (SF) : the successor can finish only after the predecessor has started.
Predecessor starts → then successor can finish.

Type	 Predecessor	   Successor	   Controls
FS	  finishes	      then starts	   Successor’s start
SS	  starts	        then starts	   Successor’s start
FF	  finishes	      then finishes	 Successor’s finish
SF	  starts	        then finishes	 Successor’s finish


Dependency = waiting for a task.
Constraint = waiting for a date.

                                                                Task constraint

A task constraint is a rule about one task’s dates.   Constraint = a date rule on a single task.
What the constraint fixes : You add a constraint to the task: “Don’t start before Nov 1.

The map: three families
Family	                         Types	                    What they do
Flexible (no date typed in)	    ASAP, ALAP, EAT, LAT	      Workfront decides the dates from the plan
Date limits (you type a date)  	SNET, SNLT, FNET, FNLT    	Set a “not earlier than” or “not later than” limit
Exact dates (you type a date) 	MSO, MFO,Fixed Dates	      Pin the task to a precise date


As Soon As Possible	ASAP : As early as possible
Workfront places the task’s start as early as it can, as close to the beginning of the project as possible.

As Late As Possible	ALAP	:As late as possible
Workfront places the task as close to the end of the project as it can, so the task is done as late as the plan allows.

Earliest Available Time	EAT :
the task starts at the earliest open point after its predecessors have been considered.

Latest Available Time	LAT :	
Schedules a task to begin at the latest available time after considering predecessor-successor relationships in the project.

Start No Earlier Than	SNET	: 
the task can’t start before the date you set. Example Doors open no earlier than 10 a.m.” Opening at 10:00 or 10:30 is fine. Opening at 9:00 is not.

Start No Later Than	SNLT	:
the task must start on or before the date you set. It can’t start after that date. The bus leaves at 8. You must board no later than 8

Finish No Earlier Than	FNET :
the task can’t finish before the date you set

Finish No Later Than	FNLT	: 
the task can’t finish after the date you set

Must Start On	MSO	 :
the task must start exactly on the date you set. Not earlier, not later.

Must Finish On	:
the task must finish exactly on the date you set. Not earlier, not later.

Fixed Dates	FIXT	: 
Schedules a task to start and end on specific dates.

Family	      Types	Date               typed by you?
Flexible	    ASAP, ALAP, EAT, LAT	   No
Date limits   SNET, SNLT, FNET, FNLT	 Yes
Exact dates	  MSO, MFO, FIXT	         Yes



                                                                Task duration

A duration is how long a task takes to complete, from its start to its finish.exclude weekends, holidays and time off. They count working time only.
Duration units : A duration needs a unit, which says what kind of time the number means. “4” alone is not enough. Is it 4 days, 4 hours or 4 weeks?

Letter	  Unit
M       	Minutes
H        	Hours
D	        Days   Days is the default.
W       	Weeks
MO (or T)	Months

How the duration and the dates work together
A task has three connected numbers: a start date, a duration and a finish date. If you know two of them, Workfront can work out the third.
Start + duration → finish

Elapsed duration
Elapsed duration counts every calendar day, including weekends and holidays. It is time passing on the clock, not working time.

Regular duration = working time only.
Elapsed duration = all calendar time.

Unit 
EM(minute), EH(Hour), ED(Day), EW(Week), ET(month)

Duration       entered	   How it counts	             Finish
2D (regular)	 Fri,       then Mon (weekend skipped)	 Monday
2ED (elapsed)	 Fri,       then Sat (weekend counted)	 Saturday


