                                                     Lag


lag is the time that must pass between a predecessor’s end and the start of the task that depends on it.
Lag = a waiting gap between two linked tasks.
Lag	Effect	Written as
Positive	Successor starts after a gap	2FS+4d
Negative	Successor can start before the predecessor ends (overlap)	2FS-2d

 lag types


Type	          Code	         How the gap is counted
Days (default)	d	             Working days. Weekends don’t count.
Calendar Days 	c	             Every day, including weekends and holidays(Saturday and Sunday)
Percent	        p              A percentage of the predecessor’s duration  ( start the next phase once the first one is half done)(2fs+25p) means after 25%work done on predecessor
Day of Week    	w	             the number you type is a weekday number, not a count of days.  4fs+7w it means 7w(saturday)
Day of Week Non Zero	k	       Like Day of Week, but the lag can never be zero. The successor never starts on the same day the predecessor finishes. +5k	The lag can’t be zero, so it moves to the adjacent week’s Thursday.




                                                  Critical path, 

A critical task is a task where a delay moves the project’s finish date.

Definition: “The critical path is the sequence of tasks that determines the project’s finish date. Workfront flags these tasks automatically.”
Why: “It shows which tasks can delay the project, so the manager knows where to focus.”
Delay: “If a critical task slips, the project’s finish date moves by the same amount.”
Float: “Float is how long a task can slip without delaying the project. Critical tasks have none.” (It is like spare time)
Where: “In the Gantt chart, through the Options icon and Critical Path, where critical tasks get a red line. And in the task list, as a Critical Path flag in the Flags column.”
Needs: “Tasks with durations and dependencies, because the path is calculated from them.”

